{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>A servant of servants shall he be to his brothers.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He cursed him measure for measure, for he dishonored his father when he looked at his nakedness, and not only did he not cover him but he told of it outside, that is, he mocked. And it is well known that a slave is despised, therefore he cursed him with servitude, that he should be despised all his days, in place of his having despised his father. And concerning Shem and Japheth, who covered their father's nakedness and placed the garment upon both their shoulders in a manner of honor — the Holy One, blessed be He, fixed their reward measure for measure: Israel, who received the Torah, who are of the descendants of Shem, merited the commandment of tzitzit; Gog and Magog, who are of the descendants of Japheth, in the time to come of the Messiah shall merit burial, that they shall be covered and buried — Gog and Magog of the descendants of Japheth, as it says (Genesis 10:2): ״בני יפת גומר ומגוג״. They shall merit burial, as it says (Ezekiel 39:11): ״אתן לגוג מקום שם קבר״." "<b>And from here</b> it is possible to prove that when two people perform one commandment together, even though the commandment is one, this one receives more reward than that one when he takes initiative and exerts himself more in its performance than his fellow. And this is what it says: ויקח שם ויפת — 'Shem and Japheth took' — and it does not say ויקחו — 'they took'; this hints to you that Shem exerted himself to perform the commandment more, and therefore the reward of the commandment of tzitzit was fixed for him, which is greater than the reward of burial. And similar to this they said in tractate Sotah, chapter 'And these are said': Joseph, who sanctified the Name in private, had a letter from the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, added to his name, as it says (Psalms 81:6): ״עדות ביהוסף שמו״; Judah, who sanctified the Name in public, was called entirely by the name of the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'shall he be to his brothers' — thirty families, corresponding to the numerical value of יהי״ה, went forth from Canaan, and they are those mentioned in the portion. And the law of a Canaanite slave: silver, thirty shekels, shall be given to his master."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:35.690450077Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}